※ pl4g-test: compile=fail
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4243 LANG_WIDE_MUL_NOT_A_WORD
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4243 LANG_WIDE_MUL_NOT_A_WORD
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4206 LANG_TYPE_OPERAND_MISMATCH

※ `⎕wide_mul` answers the product nothing else has room for, which is the one
※ of two 64-bit numbers.  Two narrower numbers have a product a wider type
※ holds, so they are widened and multiplied the ordinary way; a floating-point
※ number has no halves to answer; and the two are of one type, as the
※ operands of every arithmetic operator are.

let std := ⎕import("std")

let a: u32 = 7u32
let x: f64 = 2.5f64
let w: u64 = 3u64
let s: i64 = 3i64

@[startup]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    let h, l := ⎕wide_mul(a, a)
    let p, q := ⎕wide_mul(x, x)
    let m, n := ⎕wide_mul(w, s)
    0u6
